                  "text": "# File Organizer CLI\n\n## Description\n\nFile Organizer CLI is a simple command-line tool that sorts files in a given\nfolder into categorized subfolders based on their file extensions.\nIt currently supports the following categories and extensions:\n\n- **Images** \u2014 `.jpg`, `.jpeg`, `.png`, `.gif`\n- **Documents** \u2014 `.pdf`, `.txt`, `.docx`\n- **Videos** \u2014 `.mp4`, `.mov`, `.avi`\n\nFiles whose extensions do not match any category are left in place.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- **Python 3.x** (3.6 or later)\n\nNo external packages are required. The script uses only the standard-library\nmodules `os`, `shutil`, and `sys`.\n\n## Usage\n\nRun the script from your terminal:\n\n```bash\npython organizer.py\n```\n\nThe script will prompt you for the path of the folder to organize. A typical\nsession looks like this:\n\n```bash\n$ python organizer.py\nEnter the path of the folder to organize: ./test_folder\nMoved photo.jpg to Images/\nMoved report.pdf to Documents/\nMoved clip.mp4 to Videos/\nMoved notes.txt to Documents/\n```\n\nAfter running, the folder will contain the subfolders `Images/`, `Documents/`,\nand `Videos/` (created automatically if they do not already exist), each\nholding the files that matched the corresponding category.\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\nIntro_to_AI_mini_proj2/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 organizer.py      # Main script \u2013 the file organizer\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 test_folder/      # Example folder for testing\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Images/       # (created on first run)\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Documents/    # (created on first run)\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 Videos/       # (created on first run)\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md         # This file\n```",
                  "path": "/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_mini_proj2/README.md"
                },
                "truncated": false
              },
              "output": "<path>/home/jovyan/Intro_to_AI_mini_proj2/README.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # File Organizer CLI\n2: \n3: ## Description\n4: \n5: File Organizer CLI is a simple command-line tool that sorts files in a given\n6: folder into categorized subfolders based on their file extensions.\n7: It currently supports the following categories and extensions:\n8: \n9: - **Images** \u2014 `.jpg`, `.jpeg`, `.png`, `.gif`\n10: - **Documents** \u2014 `.pdf`, `.txt`, `.docx`\n11: - **Videos** \u2014 `.mp4`, `.mov`, `.avi`\n12: \n13: Files whose extensions do not match any category are left in place.\n14: \n15: ## Requirements\n16: \n17: - **Python 3.x** (3.6 or later)\n18: \n19: No external packages are required. The script uses only the standard-library\n20: modules `os`, `shutil`, and `sys`.\n21: \n22: ## Usage\n23: \n24: Run the script from your terminal:\n25: \n26: ```bash\n27: python organizer.py\n28: ```\n29: \n30: The script will prompt you for the path of the folder to organize. A typical\n31: session looks like this:\n32: \n33: ```bash\n34: $ python organizer.py\n35: Enter the path of the folder to organize: ./test_folder\n36: Moved photo.jpg to Images/\n37: Moved report.pdf to Documents/\n38: Moved clip.mp4 to Videos/\n39: Moved notes.txt to Documents/\n40: ```\n41: \n42: After running, the folder will contain the subfolders `Images/`, `Documents/`,\n43: and `Videos/` (created automatically if they do not already exist), each\n44: holding the files that matched the corresponding category.\n45: \n46: ## Project Structure\n47: \n48: ```\n49: Intro_to_AI_mini_proj2/\n50: \u251c\u2500\u2500 organizer.py      # Main script \u2013 the file organizer\n51: \u251c\u2500\u2500 test_folder/      # Example folder for testing\n52: \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Images/       # (created on first run)\n53: \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 Documents/    # (created on first run)\n54: \u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 Videos/       # (created on first run)\n55: \u2514